From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: the display engine's clock, bus gate and reset

The display engine is the last block on the DisplayPort path with nothing in
the CCU. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` gave the transmitter its bus gate, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` the pixel clock and the
two VIDEO_OUT1 gates, and both stopped at the TCON; neither models a DE clock. This patch is the DE's half: DE0_CLK_REG
0x0A00, DE0_BGR_REG 0x0A04, and PLL_DE, which is here because DE0_CLK's
seven-way source mux names its two taps at indices 0 and 1 -- and index 0 is
the mux's reset default.

New clocks: pll-de, de-pll4x, de-pll3x, de0, bus-de0 (ids 69..73). New reset:
RST_BUS_DE0 = 27. Nothing references any of them until the device tree in the
next patch does.

⚠ THE DESIGN CHANGED AFTER BOARD READINGS, AND THIS HEADER IS THE SECOND ANSWER.
The first cut modelled PLL_DE read-only -- no enable bit anywhere -- and pinned
the engine to PERI0_480M instead, on the grounds that PLL_DE feeds the SoC
interconnect. Measurement retired that. This patch now models PLL_DE the way
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` models the video PLLs: real clk_ops, the enable under the framework, rate
propagation on, and the device tree pinning the engine to DEPLL3X, which is both
the mux's reset default and what the vendor uses. The old reasoning is deleted
rather than struck through; what replaced it is below.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
Register geometry: the CCU field tables of the A733 user manual (A733 UM
V0.92 4.1.6), each field cross-read against the UM text, CCU base 0x02002000 from the memory map of the A733 user manual. Absolute addresses are given
alongside offsets throughout: an offset read against the wrong base returns a
plausible number rather than an error, which has already cost one session here.

  DE0_CLK_REG    0x0A00 / abs 0x02002A00  reset 0x0000_0000
    31     DE0_CLK_GATING   0=OFF 1=ON
    26:24  CLK_SRC_SEL      000 DEPLL3X    001 DEPLL4X    010 PERI0_480M
                            011 PERI0_400M 100 PERI0_300M 101 VIDEO0PLL4X
                            110 VIDEO2PLL4X
    4:0    FACTOR_M         M = FACTOR_M + 1, field 0..31
    DE0_CLK = Clock Source / M
  DE0_BGR_REG    0x0A04 / abs 0x02002A04  reset 0x0000_0000, bit16 DE0_RST,
                 bit0 DE0_GATING
  PLL_DE_CTRL_REG 0x02E0 / abs 0x020022E0 reset 0x4012_5600
    DEPLL4X = InputFreq * N/M1/P0, DEPLL3X = InputFreq * N/M1/P1
    P0 = PLL_OUTPUT0_DIV[22:20] + 1, field 0..7
    P1 = PLL_OUTPUT1_DIV[18:16] + 1, field 0..7
    N  = PLL_N[15:8] + 1, field 0..254, field must be at least 11
    M1 = PLL_INPUT_DIV[1] + 1, field 0..1
    VCO 1.26G..2.52G, default 2.088G, P0/P1 1..8, Table 4-2
  AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG 0x05C0 / abs 0x020025C0 bit5 DE_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG,
                 reset 1
  interrupt: INTID 82 "DE" = GIC_SPI 50

Silicon. Three states, and between them they settle every question this patch
had to answer.

  (a) COLD, OUR OWN BOOT CHAIN, at the U-Boot prompt after a power-cycle --
      nothing had run:
        PLL_DE_CTRL   abs 0x020022E0 = 0x41125600  PLL_EN 0 -> the PLL is OFF,
                                                   at the UM's reset factors
                                                   (N 87, VCO 2088, DEPLL3X 696)
        DE0_CLK       abs 0x02002A00 = 0x00000000  gate off, mux 000 = DEPLL3X
        DE0_BGR       abs 0x02002A04 = 0x00000000  bus gate off, reset asserted
        NSI_CLK       abs 0x02002580 = 0xC3000000  CLK_SEL 011 = PERI0_600M
        DEPLL_GATE_EN abs 0x02003928 = 0x00030003  exactly the UM default
      ⚠ So OUR boot chain does not touch PLL_DE at all. It hands Linux the
      documented reset value plus the reserved bit 24, which the 2026-08-19
      U-Boot session already found set on PLL_VIDEO1 and concluded is a
      power-on value.

  (b) THE VENDOR SYSTEM WITH ITS DISPLAY ENGINE GENUINELY LIVE -- DE version
      word 0x05008014 = 0x03520100, VI channel 0 scanout 0x05101018 =
      0xFC000000, picture on the monitor:
        PLL_DE_CTRL   abs 0x020022E0 = 0xFD124A00  PLL_EN and LOCK set,
                                                   N 75 -> VCO 1800,
                                                   DEPLL3X 600 MHz
        DE0_CLK       abs 0x02002A00 = 0x80000000  gate on, mux 000 = DEPLL3X,
                                                   FACTOR_M 0 -> DIVISOR M = 1
        DEPLL_GATE_EN abs 0x02003928 = 0x00030000  SW gates still permissive,
                                                   both AUTO_GATE_EN cleared
      and every other block the UM lists on PLL_DE, read in the same session:
        NSI      abs 0x02002580 = 0xC3000000  011 -> PERI0_600M  (DEPLL3X = 101)
        DRAM0    abs 0x02002C00 = 0x80000003  000 -> DDRPLL      (DEPLL3X = 011)
        VE_ENC0  abs 0x02002A80 = 0x01000000  001 -> VE1PLL      (DEPLL3X = 101)
        VE_DEC   abs 0x02002A88 = 0x01000000  001 -> VE0PLL      (DEPLL3X = 101)
        NPU      abs 0x02002B00 = 0x00000000  000 -> NPUPLL      (DEPLL3X = 110)
      ⇒ NOT ONE of them is on DEPLL3X. DE0 is the only block on this PLL.

  (c) the 6 GiB board under the vendor stack, 2026-07-18, eDP link live but the display
      engine OFF (DE0_CLK and DE0_BGR both 0): PLL_DE_CTRL = 0x05124a00,
      PLL_EN clear.

WHAT WAS WRONG, AND WHAT IS TRUE INSTEAD
-----------------------------------------
This patch's first cut modelled PLL_DE with no enable bit and pinned the engine
to PERI0_480M. Three claims held that up. All three are deleted:

  1. "PLL_DE feeds NSI, the SoC interconnect, so touching it hangs the chip."
     The UM row "PLL_DE: NSI, DE0, VE, NPU, DRAM0, CSI" is a
     map of what MAY be sourced from this PLL, not of what is. Every consumer on
     it reaches DEPLL3X through a mux with other inputs, DE0 is the only one
     whose mux RESETS to it, and reading (b) shows none of the other five is on
     it while the display runs. NSI reads the same 0xC3000000 cold, so the
     answer does not depend on whose operating system is up. The UM's own
     statement is conditional -- it recommends DEPLL3X as the NSI source only
     when NSI_CLK runs at 700 MHz (A733 user manual) -- and this board does not take that option.
  2. "PLL_DE is measurably off on a running board." That was a STATE offered as
     a property. It is off cold (a) and off on the 6 GiB board with the DE off (c), and on
     and locked when the DE is scanning (b). Its enable tracks the display
     engine.
  3. "The UM tells a 2K mode to use another source". True as
     text, and it is the consequence of the sentence before it: NSI at 700 MHz
     means DEPLL3X must not be retuned, so a 2K DE that wants less has to look
     elsewhere. With NSI measured off DEPLL3X the premise does not hold here,
     and the PLL can simply be tuned to what the mode needs -- which is exactly
     what the vendor does, running it at 600 rather than the reset 696.

WHAT READING (a) SHOWS, AND WHY IT ARGUES FOR OWNING THE PLL RATHER THAN AVOIDING IT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The concern behind the first cut was right for our path even though its
diagnosis was not. Cold, our chain hands Linux a DE whose mux already points at
DEPLL3X and a DEPLL3X that is OFF. A driver that ungates DE0 and leaves the
clock tree alone therefore gets a stopped clock and reads 0x00000000 out of the
whole 4 MiB aperture with its gate and its reset both correct -- indistinguishable
from the reset-ordering bug `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` measured on eDP.

There are exactly two ways out of that and no third: move the mux to a
peripheral tap and never own the PLL, or keep the mux where the silicon puts it
and own the PLL properly. With the interconnect hazard measured dead, the second
is the better one. It describes the block the way it is built, it is what the
vendor does, and it is what `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` already does one PLL along.

SO THIS BLOCK IS MODELLED LIKE `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`'s VIDEO PLLs
-------------------------------------------------
  pll-de     ccu_nm, .enable = BIT(31), .lock = BIT(28), CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE
  de-pll4x   PLL_OUTPUT0_DIV [22:20] gated by bit 27, CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
  de-pll3x   PLL_OUTPUT1_DIV [18:16] gated by bit 26, CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
  de0        mux + M + gate, CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT | CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT

and probe() sets PLL_LDO_EN and LOCK_ENABLE on PLL_DE, exactly as it already
does for the three video PLLs, because neither is a clock gate and both must be
set before the PLL can lock and report it (UM 4.1.4.1 Method 2 steps 1 and 4,
A733 user manual). That is the ONE register this patch writes at probe, and it
writes it on a PLL five other blocks can be muxed onto -- bounded by reading (b),
which found none of them there.

THE LOCK SEQUENCE, WHICH IS THE PART THAT NEEDED CHECKING
----------------------------------------------------------
ccu_nm_ops calls ccu_helper_wait_for_lock() from ccu_nm_set_rate() and from
nowhere else, so a PLL that is enabled but never set_rate never waits for lock.
Three things make the UM's ordering come out right here, and each is checkable
in the source rather than argued:

  - CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE on pll-de. clk_change_rate() prepares and ENABLES the
    clock before calling ->set_rate and drops it after (drivers/clk/clk.c:2418
    and :2452), so the lock poll inside ccu_nm_set_rate() runs on a PLL that is
    actually on.
  - the output gate is a separate clock -- the tap -- enabled by the consumer
    after the rate is set, which is the UM's "then open the output gate".
  - the consumer sets the rate before enabling. mainline's sun8i_mixer_probe()
    does exactly that: clk_set_rate(mod_clk, cfg->mod_rate) and only then
    clk_prepare_enable(mod_clk) (drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun8i_mixer.c:618-625).

⚠ That third one is a constraint on the DE driver, not on this file, and it is
stated in `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge`'s node comment too. A driver that enables before setting the rate
opens the tap on a PLL that may not have locked.

WHAT OWNING THIS PLL COSTS
---------------------------
Named because it is real, and because the first cut avoided it. The display path
now owns PLL_DE: a modeset retunes it, and clk_disable_unused() will clear
PLL_EN on a kernel where nothing claims it -- a behaviour this patch's first cut
could not produce, since it modelled no enable bit at all. The exposure is
bounded by reading (b): five of the six documented consumers measured off
DEPLL3X, and the sixth, CSI, has no driver and no node in this tree. PLL_EN is 0
at reset anyway (a). No CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED anywhere, as everywhere else in this
file -- an unclaimed clock is an off clock, and a refcount bug shows up as a
dark screen instead of hiding.

This is the same bargain `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` struck for PLL_VIDEO1, whose header records
CSI_MASTER as inheriting it. A future VE, NPU or DRAM driver that selects
DEPLL3X from its own mux inherits a clock the display retunes.

WHY ALL SEVEN MUX PARENTS, AND WHY THAT FORCES PLL_DE IN
--------------------------------------------------------
Same reason `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` modelled PLL_VIDEO0 and PLL_VIDEO2 for a mux it only needed one
input of: ccu_mux_helper_get_parent() returns the raw register field as a parent
index, so a partially populated mux does not fail loudly -- it reports the wrong
parent, or orphans the clock when the index exceeds num_parents. Here it is not
even a hypothetical: index 0 is the mux's RESET value and the vendor's live
board sits on it, so a model without a parent at index 0 would mis-describe the
state this hardware is actually found in. Index 111 is undocumented, nothing
writes it.

The flag pair on de0 is CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT | CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT -- the
same one `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` gives edp-tv, for the same two reasons. PROPAGATION, so that one
clk_set_rate() on the module clock configures divider, tap and PLL instead of a
driver poking CCU registers behind the framework. NO_REPARENT, because which of
the seven sources drives the engine is a pipeline decision that belongs in the
device tree: without it ccu_mux_helper_determine_rate() walks all seven parents
on every rate request, and ccu_nm_recalc_rate() reports a rate from the register
fields whether or not PLL_EN is set, so a switched-off PLL_VIDEO0 or PLL_VIDEO2
advertises a rate it cannot deliver.

Worked through for the one mode this path drives, because "it propagates" is not
a claim anyone can check. From the cold state (a) -- PLL_DE off at N = 87 -- a DE
driver asking for mainline's DE33 mod_rate of 600 MHz with the device tree's
parent pinned to DEPLL3X: ccu_div_round_rate() tries divisor 1 and asks de-pll3x
for 600 MHz; de-pll3x tries P1 = 1 (parent 600, below the 1272 floor, clamped,
rejected), P1 = 2 (1200, clamped, rejected), then P1 = 3 and asks pll-de for
1800 MHz; ccu_nm_find_best() returns N = 75, M1 = 1 exactly (24 * 75 = 1800).
Fields programmed:

  PLL_DE_CTRL  [15:8] = 0x4A, [1] = 0, [18:16] = 2
  DE0_CLK      [4:0]  = 0     (FACTOR_M = 0, i.e. DIVISOR M = 1)

which is field for field what reading (b) holds. The whole word comes out
0xF5124A00 against the vendor's 0xFD124A00 -- one bit different, bit 27
PLL_OUTPUT0_GATE, because the vendor opens both taps and we use one. That is
exactly the same one-bit prediction `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` made for PLL_VIDEO1, and the UM asks
for it: PLL_OUTPUT_GATE is set to 1 for each output clock that is used
(A733 user manual).

THE FACTORS, AND WHY pll-de IS NOT A clk_fixed_rate
----------------------------------------------------
This PLL is found at TWO DIFFERENT RATES depending on what ran before, which is
the whole case against a constant:

  state                              PLL_DE_CTRL   N    VCO      DEPLL3X
  cold, our chain, U-Boot prompt     0x41125600    87   2088     696 MHz
  vendor Linux, (b) and (c)          0x*5124A00    75   1800     600 MHz

⚠ An earlier version of this header claimed the two readings shared their low 24
bits and inferred that the BOOT CHAIN had retuned the PLL. The cold reading
refutes both halves: the low bits are 0x125600 cold against 0x124A00 under
vendor Linux, and the cold word IS the UM's documented reset value 0x40125600
(A733 user manual) plus the reserved bit 24. Our boot chain does not touch PLL_DE;
the 600 MHz state is the vendor's Linux. The shared-N observation holds only
between the two Linux-running states, and nothing in this patch depends on it.

A clk_fixed_rate would therefore be wrong in one of the two states whichever
number it hardcoded, by 16 %. ccu_nm reads the register.

Two other bit-level facts worth having, both from comparing the words: the cold
word has PLL_OUTPUT1_GATE (bit 26) CLEAR while both vendor-Linux words have it
SET, so on our cold board the DEPLL3X tap gate is shut as well as the PLL being
off -- the driver has to open both, and it does, because the tap is a gated clock
of its own. And the reserved bit 24 is set in all three states, before any
operating system runs, matching what the 2026-08-19 U-Boot session concluded for
the same bit on PLL_VIDEO1.

WHAT THE DE NEEDS FROM THE CCU AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
------------------------------------------------------------
Each already in its working state at power-on. Listed with reset values and
absolute addresses so nobody adds them "to be safe" and so a failing bring-up
knows where not to look:

  DE_SYS_BGR_REG   0x0A74 / abs 0x02002A74  reset 0x00010000 -- bit16 DE_SYS_RST
                   only, NO gate bit in this register, de-asserted at POR
                   (A733 user manual). The 6 GiB board read exactly 0x00010000.
  AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG  0x05C0 / abs 0x020025C0 bit5, reset 1.
                   The 6 GiB board read 0x110103ff, bit 5 set.
  MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG 0x05E0 / abs 0x020025E0 bit11 DESYS_MBUS_GATE_SW_CFG,
                   reset 1. The path both the scanout fetch and
                   the RCQ descriptor fetch take -- absent because already open.
  CM_DESYS_CFG_REG 0x1B04 / abs 0x02003B04 reset 0x00020000 = STATUS "Power ON",
                   MODULE_MODE 0. The 6 GiB board read that with its
                   display path up: on this SoC MODULE_MODE is not the effective
                   gate for a running block, as for CM_VO1 and CM_USB2. No power
                   domain is wired, here or in the device tree.

DEPLL_GATE_EN_REG 0x1928 / abs 0x02003928 -- a SECOND gate stage in series with
the PLL's own OUTPUT0/1_GATE: bit17 DEPLL3X_GATE_SW_CFG, bit16
DEPLL4X_GATE_SW_CFG, bits 1/0 the matching AUTO_GATE_EN, reset 0x00030003
(A733 user manual). Not modelled -- and the earlier
reason given for that, "reset is permissive so nothing writes it", was wrong in
its second half. Something does write it: the live board reads 0x00030000, i.e.
the two SW gates still at their permissive reset value and both AUTO_GATE_EN
bits CLEARED, switching the taps from the reset "No-Auto" to "Auto". That is
what the vendor does one register along for the video PLLs too -- the 6 GiB board read
VIDEOPLL_GATE_EN_REG abs 0x02003910 = 0x00770000, SW gates set and AUTO_GATE
cleared. The surviving half stands: the SW gates need no
write, which is why this driver has none. The auto-gating is a power feature
this tree does not implement.

⚠ The DE taps are not in VIDEOPLL_GATE_EN_REG 0x1910, but the DE PLL still
has a second SW gate stage: DEPLL_GATE_EN_REG above, documented in the UM and
now backed by a silicon reading.

SDM / spread spectrum is not modelled. PLL_DE has PLL_DE_PAT0_CTRL_REG 0x02E8
(abs 0x020022E8) and PLL_DE_PAT1_CTRL_REG 0x02EC (abs 0x020022EC), PAT0 reset 0,
and the vendor driver does wire them. Without .sdm, ccu_sdm_helper_is_enabled()
is false and pll-de reports the nominal N/M1 rate. If a boot chain ever enables
SSC here, the reported rate is the centre frequency rather than the average --
informational only, since nothing in this tree derives anything from it.

