An EARLIER version of this patch was HW-proven, and it is not this one. On
2026-08-19 a test build of main + `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge` + `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` was run on the 8 GiB board and the display
engine scanned a real framebuffer out of DRAM, through TCON_TV1, over the
trained DisplayPort link, onto a monitor -- the first pixels from DRAM this
project has produced.
That kernel carried the PERI0_480M cut: `de0 480000000 Hz from pll-peri0-480m,
bus-de0 200000000 Hz, both enabled`. The PLL_DE ownership below did not exist
yet. A proof of one clock arrangement is not a proof of a different one, and
inheriting it would be exactly the "it booted, therefore the patch works" error
this tree guards against.

WHAT THAT RUN DOES CARRY OVER, because it exercises code this version still has
unchanged:
  - CLK_BUS_DE0 as DE0_BGR bit 0 parented to ahb. The run reports bus-de0 at
    200000000 Hz, which is the AHB rate `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` established from the UM and from
    AHB_CLK_REG on the 8 GiB board. The parent claim is measured, not inferred.
  - RST_BUS_DE0 as DE0_BGR bit 16. The block came out of reset: its version word
    0x05008014 read 0x03520100.
  - de0 as a mux + linear M + gate, and FACTOR_M as a FIELD whose divisor is
    field + 1. The run selected mux index 010 from the device tree and ran the
    engine at 480 MHz = PERI0_480M / 1.
  - and one number that was an estimate and is now a measurement: 480 MHz is
    ENOUGH for 1920x1200. The driver logged "module clock 480000000 Hz (floor
    154000000 Hz)" and the TCON's underflow and lostwarn latches both went to
    zero once the datapath was complete. The 3.1x headroom argument this header
    used to make is no longer an argument.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CARRY, i.e. everything that changed: pll-de having an enable
bit at all, CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE, the taps' gates, rate propagation into the PLL,
the probe write of LDO_EN | LOCK_ENABLE, and the device tree pinning DEPLL3X.

⚠ THE FALLBACK, named with its evidence so nobody has to reconstruct it. If the
DEPLL3X arrangement fails on silicon, the proven configuration is two edits and
no design work: put `assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_480M>` back in
`arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge`, and drop `.enable`/`CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE` from pll-de and the gates and
CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT from the taps here. That is the cut the hardware-proven build carried. It
costs 4K and 20 % of the engine clock, and its header would have to say plainly
that it routes around a hazard that does not exist -- but it works, and that is
recorded rather than left as folklore.

WHAT WOULD PROVE IT, AS ONE OBSERVABLE. With a DE driver holding these clocks
and asking for 600 MHz:

  DE0_BGR_REG   abs 0x02002A04 = 0x00010001   reset de-asserted, gate pass
  DE0_CLK_REG   abs 0x02002A00 = 0x80000000   gate on, CLK_SRC_SEL 000 DEPLL3X,
                                              FACTOR_M 0 -> DIVISOR M = 1
  PLL_DE_CTRL   abs 0x020022E0 = 0xF5124A00   EN+LDO+LOCK_ENABLE+LOCK set,
                                              OUTPUT1_GATE (bit 26) set,
                                              OUTPUT0_GATE (bit 27) CLEAR,
                                              N 75 -> DEPLL3X 600 MHz
  and then the DE version word 0x05008014 = 0x03520100.

The first two are bit-identical to what the vendor's live board holds. The third
differs from the vendor's 0xFD124A00 by exactly bit 27, because they open both
output gates and we open only the tap we use -- the same one-bit difference `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`
predicted for PLL_VIDEO1, and the one the user manual asks for.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT, in the order the failures separate:

  - the two CCU registers reading correctly while 0x05008014 still reads
    0x00000000. The CCU echoing a write proves the write landed, not that a
    clock came out of it; the version word is on the far side of both the gate
    and the reset.
  - PLL_DE_CTRL bit 28 (LOCK) clear after a modeset. The PLL was enabled without
    locking -- which would mean the consumer enabled before setting the rate, the
    one ordering constraint this patch cannot enforce from here.
  - clk_summary, or /sys/kernel/debug/clk/de0/clk_parent, showing de0's parent as
    anything but de-pll3x. The device tree's assigned-clock-parents did not take.
  - PLL_DE_CTRL reading 0x41125600 -- the cold reset value -- after a modeset:
    nothing reached the PLL at all, i.e. propagation is not happening and only
    DE0_CLK's own divider moved.
  - DEPLL_GATE_EN_REG abs 0x02003928 reading anything but 0x00030003 on a COLD
    boot of our own kernel. We do not implement auto-gating and write nothing
    there; the vendor's 0x00030000 is that stack's doing. (Warm-rebooting out of
    the vendor system leaves the CCU as the vendor left it, so this is only
    meaningful cold.)
  - any read of NSI_CLK_REG abs 0x02002580 that is NOT 0xC3000000. The whole
    licence to own this PLL rests on the interconnect being on PERI0_600M; if a
    future boot chain moves it to DEPLL3X (index 101), this design has to go back
    to a peripheral tap.

NOTHING IS OWED. The three readings this patch asked for -- the cold state of our
own chain, NSI's source, and the rest of the PLL_DE fan-out -- have all been
taken, and they are quoted above.

